---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { SITE_URL } from "@/lib/seo";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema, buildArticleSchema } from "@/lib/schema";

const title = "JSON Formatter & Validator — Complete Guide | ToolBite";
const description = "Fix invalid JSON fast: trailing commas, single quotes, unquoted keys, and minify vs beautify explained with a practical debugging checklist.";
const canonicalPath = "/guides/json-formatter-guide.html";
const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}${canonicalPath}`;
const schemas = [
  buildArticleSchema({ title, description, url: pageUrl }),
  buildBreadcrumbSchema([
    { name: "Home", url: SITE_URL },
    { name: "Guides", url: `${SITE_URL}/guides.html` },
    { name: "JSON Formatter Guide", url: pageUrl }
  ])
];
---

<GuideLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  schemas={schemas}
  badge="Developer"
  badgeColor="#4A9FE0"
  icon="💻"
  readTime="4 min read"
>
  <!-- Main article -->
  <article class="gl-article">

    <div class="gl-box gl-box--info">
      <span class="gl-box-icon">💡</span>
      <span><strong>Best for:</strong> debugging API payloads, validating JSON-LD snippets, and cleaning malformed config files before pasting into production.</span>
    </div>

    <h2>Step-by-step debugging flow</h2>
    <p>When a JSON payload fails to parse, work through these steps in order. JSON errors cascade — fixing the first one often clears several others automatically.</p>
    <ol class="gl-steps">
      <li>Paste your raw JSON into <a href="/tools/json-formatter.html">the formatter</a> exactly as received — don't clean it first.</li>
      <li>Read the first syntax error only. The line and column number is exact.</li>
      <li>Fix that one error, then re-run validation before changing anything else.</li>
      <li>Repeat until the formatter reports no errors.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Most frequent JSON errors</h2>
    <p>These four account for the vast majority of JSON parse failures in the real world:</p>
    <ul class="gl-checklist">
      <li><strong>Trailing commas</strong> — <code>,]</code> or <code>,}</code> at the end of an array or object. JSON does not allow them; JavaScript objects do.</li>
      <li><strong>Single quotes</strong> — JSON requires double quotes (<code>"key": "value"</code>). Single-quoted strings are invalid.</li>
      <li><strong>Unquoted property names</strong> — <code>{ key: "value" }</code> is valid JavaScript but invalid JSON.</li>
      <li><strong>Comments</strong> — <code>// comment</code> and <code>/* block */</code> are not part of the JSON spec.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Beautify vs Minify — when to use each</h2>
    <div class="gl-quick-ref">
      <h3>Quick reference</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Beautify (pretty-print)</strong> — use during development and debugging. Indents nested structures for easy reading. Never put beautified JSON in production API responses unnecessarily — it wastes bandwidth.</li>
        <li><strong>Minify</strong> — removes all whitespace. Use for production payloads, inline JSON-LD in HTML, and stored config files where size matters.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>JSON-LD for SEO: extra considerations</h2>
    <p>Formatting JSON-LD structured data is not just about syntax — it also needs to match the Schema.org vocabulary. The formatter catches syntax errors but won't flag incorrect property names.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>After fixing syntax, test with <strong>Google's Rich Results Test</strong>.</li>
      <li>Ensure required properties for your schema type (<code>@type</code>, <code>name</code>, etc.) are present.</li>
      <li>Minify before embedding in a <code>&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;</code> tag.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>JSON vs YAML vs TOML for configuration</h2>
    <p>If you control the format, pick based on your needs: JSON is predictable but verbose. YAML allows comments and multi-line strings but indentation errors are silent. TOML is designed for config and handles nested structures more readably than JSON. Use JSON where tooling interoperability matters most.</p>

    <h2>Before-you-publish checklist</h2>
    <ul class="gl-checklist">
      <li>No trailing commas after the final key or array item.</li>
      <li>All property names and string values use double quotes.</li>
      <li>Objects and arrays open and close in the correct order.</li>
      <li>Minified version still validates after copy-paste.</li>
      <li>If JSON-LD: required Schema.org properties are present.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="gl-cta-row">
      <a href="/tools/json-formatter.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-primary">Open JSON Formatter →</a>
      <a href="/guides/jwt-decoder-guide.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-outline">JWT Decoder guide</a>
      <a href="/guides/base64-encoding-guide.html" class="tb-btn tb-btn-outline">Base64 guide</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <!-- Tools used -->
  <section>
    <p class="gl-section-heading">Tools used in this guide</p>
    <div class="gl-tools-row">
      <a href="/tools/json-formatter.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">💻</span> JSON Formatter</a>
      <a href="/tools/csv-to-json.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">🔄</span> CSV to JSON</a>
      <a href="/tools/jwt-decoder.html" class="gl-tool-btn"><span aria-hidden="true">🪪</span> JWT Decoder</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Related -->
  <section>
    <p class="gl-section-heading">Related guides & tools</p>
    <div class="gl-related">
      <a href="/guides/jwt-decoder-guide.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">🪪</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">JWT Decoder Guide</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">Inspect token payloads, then format decoded JSON.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/guides/base64-encoding-guide.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">📎</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">Base64 Encoding</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">What Base64 is, when to use it, and why it's not encryption.</p>
      </a>
      <a href="/categories/developer-tools.html" class="gl-card">
        <span class="gl-card-icon">🛠️</span>
        <p class="gl-card-title">All Developer Tools</p>
        <p class="gl-card-desc">JSON, JWT, UUID, hashing, Base64 and more.</p>
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
